(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-ocular-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Advanced Ocular History & Examination Quiz 1 — PD2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Eye pain WITH HEADACHE suggests what?","choices":["Conjunctivitis","Acute narrow angle glaucoma","Corneal abrasion or foreign body","Optic neuritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — eye pain together with headache is the pairing for acute narrow angle glaucoma. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the pain of acute iritis like?","choices":["Severe, aching, deep","Absent","Moderate, aching, deep","Mild discomfort rather than pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — acute iritis causes moderate, aching, deep pain, along with decreased vision. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"Which nerve mediates the pupillary constriction of the near reaction?","choices":["The oculomotor nerve; accompanied by convergence and accommodation","The optic nerve; accompanied by convergence and dilation","The trochlear nerve; accompanied by depression and intorsion","The abducens nerve; accompanied by abduction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the oculomotor nerve mediates the near constriction; convergence and accommodation happen alongside it but are not part of it. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which of these belongs in the URGENT group, seen by ophthalmology within a day or less?","choices":["Pinguecula and pterygium","Acute glaucoma","Sudden vision loss, chemical burns and suspected globe rupture","Dry eye and blepharitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — acute glaucoma is on the urgent list with orbital cellulitis, corneal ulcer or abrasion, retinal detachment and hyphema. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Corneal injury, acute iritis and glaucoma can make the eye diffusely red rather than showing ciliary injection. Which clues still point to them?","choices":["Pain, decreased vision, unequal pupils, a hazy cornea","Itch, watery discharge and lid crusting","Bilateral involvement together with a tender preauricular node","Fever and periorbital swelling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — pain, decreased vision, unequal pupils and a less than perfectly clear cornea mark these more serious disorders. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"How is the static finger wiggle test performed?","choices":["Covering and uncovering each eye in turn","Wiggling fingers brought slowly inward from beside the ears until seen","Moving a red-topped pin inward from beyond the boundary of each quadrant","Shining a light alternately into each eye"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — at arm's length, hands two feet apart beside the ears, wiggling fingers come slowly inward until seen, quadrant by quadrant. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Where does a chalazion usually point?","choices":["At the lateral canthus","Inside the lid rather than on the lid margin","On the lid margin, right at the base of the lashes","At the medial canthus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a chalazion, a nodule of a blocked meibomian gland, usually points inside the lid rather than on the lid margin. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"At what setting can an ophthalmoscope be used to look for a corneal ulcer?","choices":["Set at +40 diopters, giving roughly a ten times magnifier","Set at zero diopters — and this is more sensitive than fluorescein","Set at minus 20 diopters — with fluorescein being less sensitive","No ophthalmoscope setting is useful here"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — +40 diopters in effect fixes a 10 times magnifying lens, though fluorescein staining is more sensitive for early ulcers. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Eye pain WITH BLINKING suggests what?","choices":["Acute narrow angle glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Temporal arteritis","Corneal abrasion or a foreign body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — eye pain with blinking points to a corneal abrasion or a foreign body on the eye's surface. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A red eye with severe deep pain, a steamy cornea and a dilated fixed pupil signifies what?","choices":["Bacterial, viral and other infections, or allergy and irritation","Abrasions and other injuries; viral and bacterial infections","Often none; may result from trauma, bleeding disorders, or a sudden increase in venous pressure","An acute increase in intraocular pressure — an emergency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — severe deep pain, a steamy cornea and a fixed dilated pupil signify an acute rise in intraocular pressure, an emergency. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"Acute, unilateral and PAINLESS visual loss suggests which group of causes?","choices":["Simple glaucoma or cataract","Retinal vascular occlusion, retinal detachment, vitreous hemorrhage, macular degeneration","Corneal abrasion or ulcer, uveitis, traumatic hyphema, acute narrow angle glaucoma","Thermal, radiation or chemical exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — acute, unilateral, painless loss suggests retinal vascular occlusion, retinal detachment, vitreous hemorrhage or macular degeneration. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Nodular episcleritis is associated with which two systemic diseases?","choices":["Graves disease, myasthenia gravis and thyroid orbitopathy","Sjögren syndrome and sarcoidosis","Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus erythematosus","Diabetes and hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — nodular episcleritis can be seen in rheumatoid arthritis and lupus erythematosus. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which cause of red eye has no discharge, an unaffected pupil, a clear cornea and no pain?","choices":["Subconjunctival hemorrhage","Uncomplicated bacterial conjunctivitis","Acute iritis","Glaucoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — subconjunctival hemorrhage has absent pain and discharge, an unaffected pupil and vision, and a clear cornea. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"What must be documented when the pupils are dilated?","choices":["The time of dilation and the agents used","The visual acuity before dilation only","The patient's consent form number","Nothing specific"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — record the time of dilation and the agents used; a weak mydriatic reverses in 4 to 6 hours. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which complaints fall in the EMERGENT group, referred to ophthalmology immediately?","choices":["Sudden vision loss, retinal artery occlusion, chemical burns, rupture, acute angle-closure glaucoma, and vitreous hemorrhage","Acute glaucoma, orbital cellulitis, corneal ulcer or abrasion, retinal detachment, macular edema, and hyphema","Blepharitis, chalazion and dry eye","Conjunctivitis and subconjunctival hemorrhage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — sudden vision loss, retinal artery occlusion, chemical burns, rupture, angle closure and vitreous hemorrhage go to ophthalmology immediately. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Bilateral visual loss usually implies which kind of problem?","choices":["A primary neurologic cause rather than a primary ophthalmologic one","A primary ophthalmologic cause rather than a neurologic one","An infective cause","A traumatic cause"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bilateral visual loss usually implies a primary neurologic etiology and not a primary ophthalmologic problem. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 10 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"When must pupillary reactions be checked, relative to dilation?","choices":["After dilating","It makes no difference","Only after the fundoscopic examination","Before dilating"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pupillary reactions are checked before dilating; the weak mydriatic used for fundoscopy takes 4 to 6 hours to reverse. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Once local eye disease is excluded, which of these remains a cause of a dilated pupil?","choices":["Optic neuritis, papilledema and optic atrophy","Compression or other lesion of the third cranial nerve","Glaucoma, cataract and macular degeneration","Horner syndrome, myasthenia gravis and Graves disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three remaining causes are a third nerve lesion, ciliary ganglion damage causing Adie's pupil, and pharmacologic sphincter block. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which two structures are commonly left unexamined?","choices":["The eyelids and eyebrows","The lacrimal sac and puncta","The extraocular muscles","The cornea and the lens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — failure to examine the cornea and lens is one of the common mistakes in the eye examination. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does the SIZE of the striking object change the injury?","choices":["Object size makes no difference","Only velocity matters, not size","Larger objects transfer most of the energy to the orbital rim","Larger objects strike the globe directly; smaller objects hit the orbital rim"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — larger objects transfer most of their energy to the orbital rim, while smaller objects may strike the globe itself. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Besides faulty alignment, what causes diplopia?","choices":["Dry eye","Cataract","A neurological problem","Refractive error alone, without a neurological component"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — besides faulty alignment, diplopia comes from neurological problems: brainstem or cerebellar lesions, or extraocular muscle weakness. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"An acute, significantly dilated pupil is a medical emergency particularly when accompanied by what?","choices":["Headache or other neurologic signs","Itching or discharge — from an allergic conjunctivitis","Fever — from orbital cellulitis","Blurred vision alone — from cataract"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — with headache or neurologic signs, an acutely dilated pupil may mean uncal herniation or a posterior communicating artery aneurysm. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which description of xanthelasma is accurate?","choices":["At the lid margin — consider bacterial infection","On the bulbar conjunctiva — consider ultraviolet exposure","Along the NASAL portions of one or both eyelids — consider lipid disorders","Along the temporal portions of the lids — consider thyroid disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — xanthelasma are raised yellowish plaques along the nasal portions of the eyelids that should prompt a look for lipid disorders. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Purulent discharge suggests what?","choices":["Viral conjunctivitis","Dry eye","Bacterial infection","Allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — purulent discharge suggests a bacterial infection, while watery or mucoid discharge suggests allergy or a virus. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How is a fluorescein stain performed?","choices":["The dye stains intact epithelium and spares defects","The dye is injected intravenously and photographed with a blue flash","The dye changes the color of the aqueous humor","Orange dye is instilled and a blue light used"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — orange dye goes into the eye and a blue light shows foreign bodies or corneal damage, since bare areas take up the dye. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Which immunization status matters in eye trauma?","choices":["Hepatitis B","Tetanus","Influenza","Pneumococcal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — tetanus immunization status is important in any patient with a history of eye trauma. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How do you assess whether the eyes are truly protruding?","choices":["Palpate the globe to judge its forward position","Stand behind the seated patient and inspect from above","Sit facing the patient and compare the two eyes directly","Shine a light from the temporal side and look for a shadow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — stand behind the seated patient, look down from above, and draw the lids up to compare the corneas with the lower lids. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What happens when a mydriatic is instilled in an Argyll Robertson pupil?","choices":["Full and rapid dilation","No change at all","Paradoxical constriction","Incomplete dilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — mydriatics cause only an incomplete dilation of an Argyll Robertson pupil. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"When is globe rupture more likely in a patient with a subconjunctival hemorrhage?","choices":["When the hemorrhage is small, flat and confined to one sector","When the patient is anticoagulated","When both eyes are affected","In trauma, and when the hemorrhage encircles the entire cornea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — globe rupture is more likely after trauma and when the hemorrhage encircles the entire cornea. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the vital sign of the eye?","choices":["Pupil reactivity","Visual fields","Visual acuity","Intraocular pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — visual acuity testing is called the vital sign of the eye and comes early in the suggested order. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"}]};
